{
  "an5.23:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 5.23 ",
  "an5.23:0.2": "3. With Five Factors ",
  "an5.23:0.3": "Corruptions ",
  "an5.23:1.1": "“Mendicants, there are these five corruptions of native gold. When native gold is corrupted by these it’s not pliable, workable, or radiant, but is brittle and not completely ready for working. ",
  "an5.23:1.2": "What five? ",
  "an5.23:1.3": "Iron, copper, tin, lead, and silver. ",
  "an5.23:1.4": "When native gold is corrupted by these five corruptions it’s not pliable, workable, or radiant, but is brittle and not completely ready for working. ",
  "an5.23:1.5": "But when native gold is free of these five corruptions it becomes pliable, workable, and radiant, not brittle, and ready to be worked. ",
  "an5.23:1.6": "Then the goldsmith can successfully create any kind of ornament they want, whether a ring, earrings, a necklace, or a golden garland. ",
  "an5.23:2.1": "In the same way, there are these five corruptions of the mind. When the mind is corrupted by these it’s not pliable, workable, or radiant. It’s brittle, and not rightly immersed in samādhi for the ending of defilements. ",
  "an5.23:2.2": "What five? ",
  "an5.23:2.3": "Sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt. ",
  "an5.23:2.4": "These are the five corruptions of the mind. When the mind is corrupted by these it’s not pliable, workable, or radiant. It’s brittle, and not rightly immersed in samādhi for the ending of defilements. ",
  "an5.23:2.5": "But